ved_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"open_issues":0,"closed_issues":120,"state":"closed","created_at":"2018-11-27T04:36:03Z","updated_at":"2019-05-18T20:58:54Z","due_on":null,"closed_at":"2019-05-18T20:58:54Z"},"comments":9,"created_at":"2019-01-26T12:30:18Z","updated_at":"2019-02-07T03:55:04Z","closed_at":"2019-02-07T03:55:04Z","author_association":"MEMBER","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"Through the use of `func` sql statements can be executed. Something like\r\n```py\r\ntable = sa.Table(...)\r\nfn = getattr(sa.func, '1; drop table other_table; select max')\r\nsa.select([fn(table.c.id)])\r\nfn = getattr(sa.func, '(select count(*) from other_table), max')\r\nsa.select([fn(table.c.id)])\r\n```\r\nexecutes the valid statements\r\n```\r\nSELECT 1; drop table other_table; select max(table.id) AS \"1; drop table other_table; select max_1\" \r\nFROM table\r\nSELECT (select count(*) from other_table), max(table.id) AS \"(select count(*) from other_table), max_1\" \r\nFROM table\r\n```\r\n\r\nThese are just two examples, but it seems that the name of function is taken as is, without executing any sort of validation.\r\n\r\nMaybe `func` should validate that the name of the function is a valid database identifier?\r\n\r\nI was thinking about letting an consumer of an api I'm writing on specify a function name and wanted to check if I needed to validate it against a list of allowed functions.\r\n\r\n","closed_by":{"login":"sqlalchemy-bot","id":36047385,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2MDQ3Mzg1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/36047385?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot","html_url":"https://github.com/sqlalchemy-bot","followers_url":"https://api.git